# openOODA std Audit — x86 Dependencies

File: openOODA/audit/x86-std-audit.oot
THIS FILE IS GENERATED — DO NOT EDIT. Source: x86 deep-dive 2026-08-25.

---

## 1. Top-level

`ooda/std/` has **12,288 .oo files**. Of these, **121 files match `\b(x86|amd64|asm|sys_exec|sys_write)\b`** with strict word boundaries; **572 contain those substrings anywhere**. The x86-specific code lives mostly in `std/hw/arch_x86/` (100 files, 3,326 lines).

## 2. std/hw/arch_x86/ — the third x86 backend

`std/hw/arch_x86/` is a self-contained x86-64 toolchain written in pure openOODA. It does **not** depend on `oodac emit-x86` and is **not** called by it. It is its own parallel implementation.

| File | Lines | Role |
|---|---|---|
| `x86_abi.oo` | 81 | SysV AMD64 ABI constants (rdi/rsi/rdx/rcx/r8/r9, rax return, callee-saved set) |
| `x86_regs.oo` | 47 | Hardware register encoding constants |
| `x86_syscalls.oo` | 193 | Linux syscall table (read/write/open/mmap/brk/exit) |
| `x86_assembler.oo` | 99 | Multi-instruction code buffer with label resolution |
| `x86_encoder.oo` | 90 | Per-instruction byte encoder (mov, add, sub, cmp, jmp, syscall, push, pop, ret, etc.) |
| `x86_codegen.oo` | 198 | Source → SSA → bytes pipeline (the real codegen) |
| `x86_linker.oo` | 148 | ELF64 linker (header + program header + section concat) |
| `x86_direct_elf.oo` | 14 | Direct ELF verification entry point |
| `x86_5th.oo`..`x86_*.oo` | many | Test fixtures for ABI, syscalls, codegen, linker |
| 100+ test fixtures | ~2000 | x86_5th, x86_6th, x86_alias, x86_call, x86_cat, x86_fib, x86_float, etc. |

**This is a real, principled x86 toolchain.** The `x86_codegen.oo` does source → SSA → bytes with a real ABI, real register set, real encoder. It is more like the C emit's x86-64 sibling in `llvm_emit_*.oo` than the template engine in `oodac/oodac/x86_emit*.oo`.

## 3. Other x86-dependent std files

Beyond `std/hw/arch_x86/`, 99 other std files mention x86/asm/sys_exec. Notable:

* `std/compile/compilers/disasm_x86.oo` — x86-64 machine code disassembler (NOP, RET, SYSCALL, PUSH, POP). Used by `prove_prod_*` fixtures.
* `std/formal_proofs/prove_prod_*` — 5+ proofs that involve x86 machine code (e.g. `prove_prod_g4_compilers_disasm_x86.oo`).
* `std/compile/sys/cross_val.oo` — has `is_direct_elf: Bool` field, used by direct-ELF verification.

## 4. Recommendation

The `oodac emit-x86` path (the 22-token-matcher template engine) and `std/hw/arch_x86/x86_codegen.oo` (the principled codegen) are two parallel implementations of similar functionality. They are not connected. Tier 5 #24 (AST-based pipeline) should ideally:

* Reuse `std/hw/arch_x86/x86_codegen.oo` as the lowering target
* Replace the 22 templates with a single call into the std-level codegen
* Eliminate ~3,000 lines of duplicate work in `oodac/oodac/x86_emit*.oo`

This is the single largest consolidation opportunity in the x86 backend. It also gives the std library a "real compiler" role rather than a "test surface" role.

## 5. Specific std files that depend on the oodac emit-x86 path

None. The 22-template engine in `oodac/oodac/x86_emit*.oo` is not called from `std/`. The std library only uses the asm-level facilities (encoder, ABI, linker) and does not depend on the source-level template matcher.

This means: replacing the template engine with a real AST path is a **backwards-compatible refactor**. No std file needs to change. The `oodac emit-x86` command will keep working (or get better); the std-level `arch_x86` keeps working independently.

## 6. Tier 9 #44 — std audit, complete

No std file is currently broken by the silent-miscompile rate of `oodac emit-x86`. The std library is self-sufficient. The 50 silent-miscompile COMPILED fixtures are all user-written `ooda/fixtures/*.oo` that happen to have a `let` or `if` shape the walker doesn't recognize. None are critical to the std library itself.
